<header class="sg-head"><p class="featured-region">Anthem · 1813 · Americas</p><h1 class="sg-title">Argentine National Anthem</h1><p class="sg-local"><bdi lang="es">Himno Nacional Argentino</bdi></p><p class="sg-kid" data-say>A song written in Buenos Aires in 1813, during the fight for independence from Spain, that became Argentina's national anthem.</p></header>
<dl class="sg-facts"><div><dt>Words</dt><dd>Vicente López y Planes</dd></div><div><dt>Music</dt><dd>Blas Parera</dd></div><div><dt>Culture</dt><dd>Patriots of Buenos Aires in the wars of independence; later the Argentine Republic</dd></div><div><dt>Language</dt><dd>Spanish</dd></div><div><dt>Place</dt><dd>Buenos Aires, Argentina</dd></div><div><dt>Date</dt><dd>words adopted 11 May 1813; music composed May 1813</dd></div><div><dt>Type</dt><dd>Anthem</dd></div><div><dt>The words</dt><dd>Public domain (words by Vicente López y Planes (1813); music by Blas Parera (1813; he died in 1840))</dd></div></dl>
<div class="sg-body">
<section class="sg-sec" id="story"><h2>The story</h2><p>After the May Revolution of 1810, poets and musicians in Buenos Aires wrote a number of patriotic songs. The earliest, a &quot;Patriotic March&quot; published anonymously on 15 November 1810 in the Gazeta de Buenos Ayres, is generally believed to have words by Esteban de Luca and music by Blas Parera; it did not mention Argentina or any independent state. In mid-1812 the ruling triumvirate had the Buenos Aires Cabildo commission a national song: a text by the Franciscan friar Cayetano Rodríguez was approved on 4 August, and Parera, the music director of the local theatre, set it and conducted its premiere on 1 November.</p><p>Within a year the Assembly of Year XIII judged that song not effective enough, and the army's recent victories had made its words out of date. On 6 March 1813 several poets were invited to submit texts, and the poem by the lawyer Vicente López y Planes was chosen unanimously. On 11 May 1813 it was designated the sole national march, and it was published as a broadside three days later. Parera wrote new music within a few days. An unconfirmed tradition places the first performance on 14 May 1813 in the home of Mariquita Sánchez de Thompson; Parera conducted the official premiere in the theatre on 28 May and was paid 200 pesos. He may have shown his dislike of the new nation by quoting, in a minor key and with dissonant chords, a gesture from an anti-Napoleonic war song.</p><p>The text was fiercely pro-independence and anti-Spanish, and its outlook reached beyond Argentina to the struggles in Mexico, Central America, northern South America and Charcas, while its allegory cast not only Spanish absolutism but Spain itself as the enemy. A disputed word in one line, changed in a corrected song-sheet bearing the same date, reverses whether Buenos Aires sets itself against the other provinces or at their head; the first reading has been seen as a sign of the city's centralist views and also as a misprint. The song spread fast: within ten years it was performed throughout Argentina and also in Chile, Peru and Colombia, where it was used until those countries had anthems of their own. It was known as the &quot;Canción Patriótica Nacional&quot; and then the &quot;Canción Patriótica&quot;; the title &quot;Himno Nacional Argentino&quot; first appears on Juan Pedro Esnaola's arrangement of about 1848.</p><p>So many versions grew up that singing together became difficult. In 1860 Esnaola was commissioned to prepare an official version, with a slower tempo, a fuller texture and richer harmony. A committee's revision of 1927 that undid some of his changes was rejected after a public debate led by the newspaper La Prensa, and Esnaola's edition was confirmed as the official anthem in 1944; Decree 10302/1944 still governs it. Once hostility to Spain had faded and many Spanish immigrants had settled in the country, President Julio Argentino Roca decreed on 30 March 1900 that at official and public festivals and in schools only the first and last verses and the chorus should be sung, so no anti-Spanish text is sung today.</p><p>The anthem is performed at all official events, where those present stand and sing; radio stations play it at midnight, and on national holidays it must be played then. At international sporting events usually only the instrumental introduction, or a shortened form, is played. The rock musician Charly García caused controversy in 1990 with an idiosyncratic version on his album Filosofía barata y zapatos de goma, which broke the regulations on reproducing the anthem. Argentina celebrates National Anthem Day on 11 May.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="meaning"><h2>What it says</h2><p>A patriotic hymn that celebrates the rise of an independent nation and calls its people to hold on to their freedom. The full 1813 text is also a fierce song of the war against Spain, with victories across the continent; the parts sung today keep only the solemn, proud opening and close.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="words"><h2>The words</h2><div class="sg-show" role="group" aria-label="Show these columns"><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="orig">Spanish</button><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="en">In English</button></div><div class="sg-blocks"><div class="sg-lyrics sg-cols-2"><div class="sg-colhead" aria-hidden="true"><span class="sg-o">Spanish</span><span class="sg-e">In English</span></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">Sean eternos los laureles</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>May the laurels be eternal</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">Que supimos conseguir;</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>that we knew how to win;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">Coronados de gloria vivamos,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>crowned with glory let us live,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">Ó juremos con gloria morir.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>or let us swear to die with glory.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">Oid mortales, el grito sagrado</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Hear, mortals, the sacred cry:</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">Libertad, libertad, libertad.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Liberty, liberty, liberty.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">Oid el ruido de rotas cadenas:</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Hear the noise of broken chains:</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">Ved en trono á la noble igualdad.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>see noble equality on a throne.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">Se levanta en la faz de la tierra</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>There rises on the face of the earth</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">Una nueva gloriosa nación,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>a new and glorious nation,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">Coronada su sien de laureles,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>its brow crowned with laurels,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">Y á sus plantas rendido un león.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>and a lion yielded at its feet.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">De los nuevos campeones los rostros</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>The faces of the new champions</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">Marte mismo parece animar:</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Mars himself seems to animate:</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">La grandeza se anida en sus pechos:</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>greatness nests in their breasts:</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">A su marcha todo hacen temblar.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>at their march they make all things tremble.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">Se conmueven del Inca las tumbas</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>The tombs of the Inca are stirred,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">Y en sus huecos revive el ardor,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>and in their hollows the ardour revives,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">Lo que ve renovando á sus hijos</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>seeing renewed by its sons</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">De la patria el antiguo esplendor.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>the ancient splendour of the homeland.</span></p></div><details class="sg-more"><summary>Show all 76 lines</summary><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">Pero sierras y muros se sienten</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>But mountains and walls are felt</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">Retumbar con horrible fragor;</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>to resound with a horrible crash;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">Todo el país se conturba por gritos</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>the whole country is troubled by cries</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">De venganza, de guerra y furor.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>of vengeance, of war and of fury.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">En los fieros tiranos la envidia</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>In the fierce tyrants envy</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">Escupió su pestífera hiel;</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>spat out its pestilent gall;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">Su estandarte sangriento levantan,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>they raise their bloody standard,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">Provocando á la lid más cruel.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>provoking to the cruellest fight.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">¿No los veis sobre Méjico y Quito</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Do you not see them upon Mexico and Quito</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">Arrojarse con saña tenaz?</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>hurl themselves with stubborn rage?</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">¿Y cual lloran bañados en sangre</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>And how, bathed in blood, weep</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">Potosí, Cochabamba y la Paz?</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Potosí, Cochabamba and La Paz?</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">¿No los veis sobre el triste Caracas</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Do you not see them upon sad Caracas</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">Luto, llantos y muerte esparcir?</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>scatter mourning, weeping and death?</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">¿No los veis devorando cual fieras</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Do you not see them devouring, like wild beasts,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">Todo pueblo que logran rendir?</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>every people they manage to subdue?</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">Á vosotros se atreve, Argentinos,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Against you, Argentines, dares</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">El orgullo del vil invasor:</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>the pride of the vile invader:</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">Vuestros campos ya pisa, contando,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>he already treads your fields, reckoning,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">Tantas glorias, hollar vencedor.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>victorious, to trample so many glories.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">Mas los bravos, que unidos juraron</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>But the brave, who united swore</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">Su feliz libertad sostener,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>to uphold their happy liberty,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">Á esos tigres sedientos de sangre</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>to those tigers thirsting for blood</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">Fuertes pechos sabrán oponer.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>will know how to set strong breasts against them.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">El valiente Argentino á las armas</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>The valiant Argentine to arms</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">Corre ardiendo de brío y valor:</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>runs, burning with spirit and valour:</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">El clarín de la guerra, cual trueno</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>the bugle of war, like thunder,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">En los campos del Sud resonó.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>resounded in the fields of the South.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">Buenos Aires se pone á la frente</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Buenos Aires places itself at the head</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">De los pueblos de la ínclita unión,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>of the peoples of the illustrious union,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">Y con brazos robustos desgarran</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>and with robust arms they tear apart</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">Al Ibérico, altivo león.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>the haughty Iberian lion.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">San José, San Lorenzo, Suipacha,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>San José, San Lorenzo, Suipacha,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">Ambas Piedras, Salta y Tucumán,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>both Piedras, Salta and Tucumán,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">La Colonia, y las mismas murallas,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>La Colonia, and the very walls</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">Del tirano en la Banda Oriental,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>of the tyrant in the Banda Oriental,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">Son letreros eternos que dicen:</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>are eternal inscriptions that say:</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">Aquí el brazo Argentino triunfó;</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>here the Argentine arm triumphed;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">Aquí el fiero opresor de la Patria</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>here the fierce oppressor of the Homeland</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">Su cerviz orgullosa dobló.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>bent his proud neck.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">La victoria al guerrero Argentino</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Victory covered the Argentine warrior</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">Con sus alas brillantes cubrió,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>with its shining wings,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">Y azorado á su vista el tirano</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>and, dismayed at the sight of him, the tyrant</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">Con infamia á la fuga se dió.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>took to flight in infamy.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">Sus banderas, sus armas se rinden</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>His flags, his arms surrender</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">Por trofeos á la Libertad,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>as trophies to Liberty,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">Y sobre alas de gloria alza el pueblo</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>and on wings of glory the people raise</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">Trono digno á su gran majestad.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>a throne worthy of its great majesty.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">Desde un polo hasta el otro resuena</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>From one pole to the other resounds</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">De la fama el sonoro clarín,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>the sonorous bugle of fame,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">Y de América el nombre enseñando</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>and, showing forth the name of America,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">Les repite — ¡Mortales oid!...</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>it repeats to them: Mortals, hear!...</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">Ya su trono dignísimo abrieron</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Now their most worthy throne have opened</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">Las Provincias Unidas del Sud;</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>the United Provinces of the South;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">Y los libres del mundo responden:</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>and the free of the world reply:</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="es">Al gran pueblo Argentino, ¡Salud!</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>To the great Argentine people, hail!</span></p></div></details></div></div><div class="sg-credit"><p>Original: Vicente López y Planes (1813); the full original text, from Clemente L. Fregeiro, Vidas de argentinos ilustres (1894). Text from <a href="https://es.wikisource.org/wiki/Himno_Nacional_Argentino" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a>; public domain.</p><p>The English is the Institute&#8217;s own translation, made from the original for this page.</p></div><details class="sg-notes"><summary>Notes on the translation (6)</summary><ul><li>line 2.8: 'rendido' is 'surrendered, subdued, lying vanquished': the lion of Spain at the new nation's feet.</li><li>lines 3.7-3.8: 'Lo que ve renovando á sus hijos / De la patria el antiguo esplendor' is loosely constructed; read as 'on seeing the homeland's ancient splendour renewed by its [the Inca's] sons'. It can also be taken as 'which [the ardour] sees its sons renewing'.</li><li>lines 6.3-6.4: 'contando ... hollar vencedor' = 'counting on trampling, as victor, so many glories' (the glories of the land).</li><li>line 8.2: 'Ambas Piedras': Las Piedras, the battles at two places of that name (Banda Oriental and the Río Piedras).</li><li>line 8.4: 'Banda Oriental': the 'Eastern Bank' of the Uruguay river, present-day Uruguay.</li><li>line 10.3: 'enseñando' can be 'showing' or 'teaching'.</li></ul></details></section>
